WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.729
Untrusted or variable-length input should be validated before allocation, parsing, copying, formatting, or transformation.

2
00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:11.840
A length field in a file, message, request, or configuration value is a claim, not proof.

3
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.423
The program should check that claim against supported limits and available data.

4
00:00:16.423 --> 00:00:19.861
Reasonable maximum sizes are part of defensive resource control.

5
00:00:19.861 --> 00:00:28.263
A format may be able to express very large lengths, but the product still needs a maximum it is willing to process.

6
00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:33.228
That maximum should consider memory use, CPU time, service expectations, and failure behavior.

7
00:00:33.228 --> 00:00:35.138
Size arithmetic deserves careful review.

8
00:00:35.138 --> 00:00:37.048
Multiplication can overflow before allocation.

9
00:00:37.048 --> 00:00:41.631
Adding a terminator, header, or alignment padding can exceed a supported maximum.

10
00:00:41.631 --> 00:00:48.124
Converting between bytes, characters, elements, and protocol units can change meaning if the code is not explicit.

11
00:00:48.124 --> 00:00:51.179
Separate parsing, validation, and memory operations where practical.

12
00:00:51.179 --> 00:00:54.235
First read enough to understand the claimed size.

13
00:00:54.235 --> 00:00:57.290
Then validate it against maximums and remaining input.

14
00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.964
Only then allocate, copy, or transform data.

15
00:00:59.964 --> 00:01:03.783
This structure makes review easier and reduces partial-state problems.

16
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:07.221
A violated size assumption should be a safe failure.

17
00:01:07.221 --> 00:01:13.332
Reject the input, preserve the previous state, clean up temporary resources, and report a bounded diagnostic.

18
00:01:13.332 --> 00:01:20.970
Do not proceed with a smaller allocation or a guessed length simply because the normal path expected a different value.

19
00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:29.373
Tests and reviews should cover zero sizes, one less than maximum, exact maximum, one more than maximum, conversion boundaries, empty input, truncated

20
00:01:29.373 --> 00:01:31.283
input, and repeated small fragments.

21
00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:34.720
Boundary cases are where many buffer assumptions become visible.
